'''''Balboa v. Riera i Sola''''' (also known as the '''''El Desig'' obscenity trial''') was a landmark {{w|court case}} in [[Balboa]] in which the government prosecuted the filmmaker [[Montserrat Riera i Sola]] under the [[Public Decency Act]] of 1976 for the production and distribution of ''[[El Desig (film)|El Desig]]'' ("The Desire"), a feminist film that depicted female sexuality, same-sex attraction, and abortion. The case proceeded through four courts over the course of 1988: the [[Sant Cristòfor]] High Court dismissed the charges before trial; the case was re-filed at the [[Portblanc]] High Court in [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]], which ruled the film obscene unanimously; the defence appealed to the [[Supreme Court of Balboa]] in [[Sant Bart]], where the proceedings attracted intensive media coverage; and the Supreme Court deferred the constitutional question to the [[Constitutional Court of Balboa]], which conducted its own hearing, struck down the Public Decency Act as incompatible with the [[Twelve Guarantees]], and acquitted Riera i Sola. The ruling left Balboa without a functioning obscenity statute from 1988 to 1990, when the Act was amended and restored to force. The case became a cause célèbre that galvanised the underground film community, feminist organisations, LGBT organisations, and civil liberties groups, and drew the mainstream film industry into a public confrontation with the government over the scope of artistic freedom. The case also exposed deep divisions within Balboa between the permissive capital and the conservative interior provinces over the role of moral standards in public life.

== Background ==

{{main|El Desig (film)}}

''[[El Desig (film)|El Desig]]'' was the second film of [[Montserrat Riera i Sola]], a feminist filmmaker born in 1948 in [[Sant Bart]] who had moved to [[Sant Cristòfor]] in the early 1970s and had worked as a scriptwriter for [[Cinema Lliure]] before leaving the mainstream studio to make films that addressed subjects the mainstream would not finance. Her first film, ''[[Les Dones del Port (film)|Les Dones del Port]]'' (1981), was a documentary about the women who worked in the harbour district's informal economy, shot on a minimal budget and screened at the [[Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre]], a community arts space in the harbour-front district that had established a regular screening programme in 1985.

''El Desig'' was produced outside the mainstream studio system and was financed through personal funds and small contributions from feminist organisations and individual supporters. The film depicted the sexual and economic lives of three women in Sant Cristòfor during the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]] of the 1980s. Its explicit content — which included depictions of female desire, same-sex attraction, and abortion — was the first frank portrayal of female sexuality in a Balboan film and was regarded by the government as a violation of the [[Public Decency Act]].

=== The Public Decency Act of 1976 ===

The [[Public Decency Act]] was enacted in 1976 during the administration of President [[Miquel Torrent i Alvarado]] as part of a broader package of social legislation aimed at the conservative interior provinces. The Act prohibited the production, distribution, and exhibition of "obscene material," defined as material that "offends public morality by depicting sexual conduct in a manner that is gratuitous or without artistic, scientific, or educational merit." The Act had been passed at the request of interior provincial assemblies, particularly those of [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] and [[Nurra Province|Nurra]], where conservative social norms and the influence of the [[Balboan Catholic Church]] were strongest. The Act had not previously been used to prosecute a film, and its application to ''El Desig'' was its first significant test in a judicial proceeding.

== Procedural history ==

=== Sant Cristòfor High Court dismissal ===

The film was screened at the Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre in late 1987. The screening was attended by approximately 200 people, including members of the underground film community, feminist organisations, and the Sant Cristòfor cultural press. A report of the screening was forwarded to the Ministry of Justice by an official in the Sant Cristòfor municipal government, whose identity has not been publicly confirmed. The Ministry of Justice referred the matter to the prosecutor's office, and in January 1988 the government initiated prosecution under the Public Decency Act.

The charges were filed at the Sant Cristòfor High Court, which dismissed them before trial. The court found that the Act's definition of obscenity did not provide sufficient grounds to proceed against a film that had been screened in a private cultural venue under a regular programming schedule, and that the prosecution had not established a prima facie case that the film's content met the statutory standard of obscenity. The dismissal was a significant early victory for the defence, but it did not end the matter.

=== Re-filing at the Portblanc High Court ===

The prosecution re-filed the charges at the Portblanc High Court in [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]]. The re-filing was made possible by the fact that one of the actors in ''El Desig'' was a resident of [[Portblanc]], which established a jurisdictional basis for the case to be brought in the Tramuntana court. The choice of venue was significant: Tramuntana was one of the provinces that had requested the enactment of the Public Decency Act, and its provincial assembly had been among the most vocal in calling for the prosecution of material that it regarded as contrary to the moral standards of the interior.

=== Portblanc High Court ruling ===

The Portblanc High Court conducted a trial and ruled unanimously that the film was obscene within the meaning of the Public Decency Act. The court found that the film's depiction of sexual conduct, including its portrayal of female desire and same-sex attraction, was gratuitous and served no artistic, scientific, or educational purpose that could not have been achieved through less explicit means. The court further found that the film's inclusion of an abortion scene was designed to provoke rather than to inform. The ruling was delivered in March 1988. The defence, led by the [[Balboan Civil Liberties Union]] (BCLU), immediately appealed the ruling to the [[Supreme Court of Balboa]].

=== Supreme Court appeal ===

The appeal was heard by the Supreme Court of Balboa in [[Sant Bart]] beginning in April 1988. The proceedings were conducted as an appeal review of the Portblanc High Court's record, with both sides presenting legal arguments on the application of the Public Decency Act and the scope of artistic freedom under the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. The Supreme Court proceedings attracted the most intensive media coverage of any court case in Balboa since reunification, with journalists from Sant Cristòfor and the interior provinces attending every hearing session.

During the proceedings, the Supreme Court justices recognised that the case raised a substantial constitutional question — whether the Public Decency Act's definition of obscenity was compatible with the Twelve Guarantees' protections for freedom of expression. In July 1988, the Supreme Court deferred the constitutional question to the [[Constitutional Court of Balboa]], which had exclusive jurisdiction over matters involving the interpretation and enforcement of the Twelve Guarantees.

== Arguments ==

=== Prosecution ===

The prosecution was led by [[Francesc Bauzá i Mas]], a deputy prosecutor from the Ministry of Justice. The charges were brought against Riera i Sola as the film's director and producer, and against the Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre as the venue where the film had been exhibited. The Cultural Centre charges were subsequently dropped after the Centre's management demonstrated that the screening had been conducted under its regular programming schedule and that the Centre had not been involved in the film's production.

Bauzá i Mas argued that the film's depiction of sexuality was gratuitous and that its distribution constituted a violation of the Public Decency Act's provisions against the dissemination of obscene material. He argued that the film's scenes of sexual conduct, including its depiction of female desire and same-sex attraction, served no narrative or artistic purpose that could not have been achieved through less explicit means, and that the film's inclusion of an abortion scene was designed to provoke rather than to inform. The prosecution called two witnesses: a professor of literature from the [[Universitat de Sant Cristòfor]] who testified that the film's artistic merit was marginal, and a representative of the [[Archdiocese of Sant Bart]] who testified that the film's content was contrary to the moral values of the Balboan interior.

=== Defence ===

The defence was led by the BCLU, a civil rights organisation that had been established during the mandate period to advocate for the enforcement of the Twelve Guarantees. The lead defence attorney was [[Magda Vives i Cardó]], the BCLU's legal director. The defence argued that the film was a legitimate artistic expression protected by the Twelve Guarantees' provisions on freedom of expression and that the Public Decency Act was an unconstitutional restriction on artistic freedom.

Vives i Cardó argued that the film's explicit content was integral to its artistic purpose: the film's depiction of female sexuality was not gratuitous but was the subject of the film itself, and the film's exploration of the private lives of its characters was a legitimate artistic endeavour that the Twelve Guarantees' protections for freedom of expression were designed to shield. The defence called three witnesses: [[Jordi Molins i Castell]], the mainstream satirical filmmaker, who testified that the film's artistic merit was evident and that the prosecution's standard for "gratuitous" content, if accepted, would endanger a wide range of Balboan films; [[Kwame Rovira]], the Black Balboan subcultural filmmaker, who testified that the underground film community had been producing films on similar subjects for years without prosecution and that the government's selective prosecution of ''El Desig'' was politically motivated; and a professor of constitutional law from the [[Balboan Jesuit University]] who testified that the Public Decency Act's definition of obscenity was unconstitutionally vague under the Twelve Guarantees.

== Constitutional Court ruling ==

The Constitutional Court of Balboa, seated in [[Sant Cristòfor]], conducted its own hearing on the constitutional question in August 1988. The court heard arguments from both sides on whether the Public Decency Act's definition of obscenity was compatible with the Twelve Guarantees. The [[Adonian Community]]'s human rights directorate filed an amicus brief with the court, arguing that the Act was incompatible with the Guarantees and that the AC retained the authority to challenge Balboan laws that violated them. The brief was significant because it represented the first intervention by the AC in a domestic Balboan court case and because it signalled that the international community was monitoring the case's outcome.

The court delivered its ruling on 22 September 1988. The Constitutional Court held that the Public Decency Act was incompatible with the Twelve Guarantees' provisions on freedom of expression, as its standard for "gratuitous" content provided no objective standard by which a court could distinguish between artistic and non-artistic depictions of sexuality. The court struck down the Act in its entirety. The court further held that the government's selective prosecution of ''El Desig'' — the first prosecution under the Act — demonstrated the statute's potential for arbitrary application. The court ruled that the film, while explicit, was a legitimate artistic expression protected by the Guarantees, and acquitted Riera i Sola. The ruling was final. The striking down of the Act left Balboa without a functioning obscenity statute; from 1988 to 1990, the Public Decency Act continued to be enforced against more blatant, non-artistic forms of obscenity, but no artistic work could be prosecuted under it. The Act was amended in 1990 to restore it to force, with its scope narrowed to exclude works of artistic, scientific, or educational merit.

== Media coverage and public reaction ==

The case generated the most intensive media coverage of any court case in Balboa since reunification. The Sant Cristòfor press covered the Supreme Court proceedings in detail from the first hearing in April 1988, and the interior provincial press followed the proceedings closely, particularly after the Portblanc High Court's obscenity ruling.

=== Sant Cristòfor press ===

''[[El Faro del Istmo]]'', the newspaper of record, provided the most extensive coverage, with daily trial reports from Sant Bart and editorial commentary that generally supported the defence. The newspaper's cultural critic, [[Eulàlia Camps i Verdaguer]], published a series of essays arguing that the case was a test of the Twelve Guarantees' commitment to freedom of expression and that a conviction would establish a precedent that could be used against any film that challenged the social norms of the interior. ''[[La Veu de la Badia]]'', the centre-left daily, editorially supported the defence and published opinion pieces by feminist and civil liberties writers. ''[[La Voz del Istmo]]'', the Creole-language weekly, published a special edition on the case that included interviews with Riera i Sola, Vives i Cardó, and members of the underground film community.

=== Television ===

[[Telebadia]], the public broadcaster, covered the Supreme Court proceedings in its evening news programme and broadcast a documentary on the underground film community in May 1988 that included excerpts from ''El Desig'' and interviews with Riera i Sola and other underground filmmakers. The documentary was controversial: the Ministry of Justice issued a statement criticising the broadcast, and several BPP members of the National Assembly called for the documentary to be withdrawn. Telebadia's editorial independence, guaranteed by the public broadcasting statute, prevented the government from acting on the calls.

[[Canal Nord]], the commercial Volisanian-language broadcaster, covered the proceedings in its news programming and broadcast a panel discussion on the Public Decency Act in June 1988 that included Vives i Cardó, a representative of the Archdiocese of Sant Bart, and a professor of constitutional law. The panel discussion was the most-watched current affairs programme of the week.

=== Interior provincial press ===

The interior provincial press generally supported the prosecution. Newspapers in [[Sant Bart]], [[Portblanc]], and [[Novara]] published editorials arguing that the film was obscene and that the Public Decency Act was a necessary protection of the moral standards of the interior provinces. The Portblanc High Court's unanimous obscenity ruling was welcomed by the Tramuntana press as a vindication of the province's moral standards. The [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] Provincial Assembly passed a resolution in May 1988 supporting the prosecution and calling on the government to "defend the moral standards of the Balboan people." The [[Nurra Province|Nurra]] Provincial Assembly passed a similar resolution in June. The [[Jala Province|Jala]] Provincial Assembly, divided between the conservative establishment of Sant Bart and the BPP-Socialist working-class districts, did not pass a resolution but debated the matter extensively.

=== The Balboan Catholic Church ===

The [[Balboan Catholic Church]] through the [[Archdiocese of Sant Bart]] issued a statement in April 1988 supporting the prosecution and arguing that the film's content was "contrary to the moral values of the Balboan people." The Archdiocese's testimony at the Portblanc trial, provided by a representative who was not a party to the case, was the most visible intervention by the Church in a cultural matter since reunification. The Church's position was criticised by the Sant Cristòfor press and by civil liberties organisations, which argued that the Church was seeking to impose the moral standards of the interior on the entire country.

=== Demonstrations ===

Demonstrations were held outside the Supreme Court in Sant Bart during the appeal proceedings. The first demonstration, organised by the defence committee on the opening day of the Supreme Court hearings in April 1988, drew approximately 500 people and was attended by members of the underground film community, feminist organisations, LGBT organisations, and civil liberties groups. Subsequent demonstrations were held at weekly intervals throughout the proceedings, with attendance growing to approximately 2,000 by June 1988. A counter-demonstration in support of the prosecution, organised by conservative interior provincial groups, drew approximately 300 people in July 1988. Additional demonstrations were held outside the Constitutional Court in Sant Cristòfor during the court's hearing in August 1988.

== Aftermath ==

=== Amendment of the Public Decency Act ===

The Constitutional Court's striking down of the Public Decency Act in September 1988 left Balboa without a functioning obscenity statute. During the two-year period from 1988 to 1990, the Act continued to be enforced against more blatant, non-artistic forms of obscenity — particularly pornography and explicit material distributed without any claim to artistic merit — but no artistic work could be prosecuted. The gap was closed in 1990, when the Act was amended by the Muntaner i Ferrer administration to restore it to force. The amendment redefined "obscene material" to exclude works of artistic, scientific, or educational merit and required that any prosecution under the Act be preceded by a judicial determination of artistic merit. The amended Act effectively rendered the statute unenforceable against films, books, and other artistic works, while preserving its application against non-artistic obscene material.

=== Cultural Defence Coalition ===

The defence committee that had been organised to support Riera i Sola during the case — which included representatives of the underground film community, the mainstream industry, feminist organisations, LGBT organisations, and civil liberties groups — reconstituted itself after the ruling as the [[Cultural Defence Coalition]], a permanent advocacy organisation dedicated to defending artistic freedom and freedom of expression in Balboa. The Coalition became a significant force in Balboan cultural life and played a role in the subsequent campaigns for the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sexual Orientation Protections]] and the first [[Pride parade|Pride march]] in Sant Cristòfor in 1993.

=== Impact on cinema ===

The ruling removed the legal threat that had constrained the underground film community and is widely regarded as a catalyst for the production of more confrontational films in the late 1980s and early 1990s. The first significant film produced in the aftermath of the case was ''[[Els Amagats (film)|Els Amagats]]'' ("The Hidden Ones", 1989), directed by [[Adrià Pons i Riera]], which depicted the lives of three LGBT individuals in Sant Cristòfor and was the first Balboan film to depict LGBT lives with frankness. The case also contributed to the partial convergence of the mainstream and underground film industries, as the mainstream's support for the defence — driven by self-interest as much as by solidarity — established a precedent for cooperation that would continue in the post-trial years.

=== Film preservation ===

''El Desig'' is preserved at the [[Arxiu Nacional de Cinema de Balboa]] as a cultural artefact of foundational significance. The film was added to the national archive in 1995, following the closure of the Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre and the dissolution of several underground production companies during the [[1994 Balboa spot recession|1994 spot recession]].

== Significance ==

The ''El Desig'' case is widely regarded as a landmark in the development of Balboan civil liberties. The Constitutional Court's ruling established that the Twelve Guarantees' protections for freedom of expression extended to artistic content that challenged social norms, and it removed the legal constraints that had inhibited the underground film community. The case is also regarded as a turning point in the relationship between the Sant Cristòfor cultural establishment and the conservative interior: the prosecution, driven by the interior's moral standards and pursued through a Tramuntana court, was ultimately defeated by the constitutional framework of the reunified state, which the Constitutional Court held could not permit the interior's standards to be enforced against the capital's artistic community. The case exposed deep divisions within Balboa over the role of moral standards in public life — divisions between the permissive, cosmopolitan capital and the conservative, religiously influenced interior provinces — that the case's outcome did not resolve but rather brought into sharp public focus. The two-year period during which the Public Decency Act had been struck down, with no functioning obscenity statute in force, further underscored the practical consequences of these divisions, as the government was forced to navigate between the interior's demands for moral regulation and the capital's insistence on constitutional protections for artistic expression.

